# JavaScript (QuickJS)

First-party runtime JS lives under `src/plotly_print/js/`. It runs inside QuickJS via `plotly_print.engine`, not in Node or a browser.

## Do not edit

- `plotly_comprehensive_2d.js` and any other vendored Plotly/plotly.js bundle. Treat as opaque third-party input.
- Only change first-party files such as `micro_dom.js`.

## Style (match `micro_dom.js`)

- IIFE + `'use strict'`. No ES modules, `import`/`export`, or build step.
- Prefer ES5 that QuickJS already accepts here: `var`, `function`, prototypes. Use newer features only when the file already does (e.g. `Proxy` with a fallback).
- 4-space indent. ASCII only in identifiers and comments (no emojis, arrows, smart dashes).
- No TypeScript. No Prettier/ESLint in this repo; match existing formatting by eye.
- Same comment rules as the rest of the project: why not what; `ponytail:` for deliberate ceilings.

## DOM / SVG stubs

- Implement the minimum Plotly/d3 actually call. Prefer attribute-backed getters (e.g. `transform` from `getAttribute('transform')`) over inventing full SVG DOM types.
- When adding a browser API stub, grep the Plotly bundle for the call shape (`baseVal.consolidate`, etc.) and return what that path reads.
- Keep geometry helpers (`parseTransform`, path flatten, bbox) shared; do not duplicate matrix math per method.

## Tests

- JS has no separate test runner. Cover behavior from Python with `quickjs.Context` loading `micro_dom.js` (see `tests/test_svg_*.py`), or via `plotly_print.to_svg` / figure fixtures when the bug is Plotly-facing.
- After non-trivial micro-DOM changes, run the relevant `tests/test_svg_*.py` (or the smallest Plotly render check that would fail).
